  "final": {
    "evidence_sufficiency": "SUFFICIENT",
    "confidence": 92,
    "conclusion": "Nominally identical questions were stable at the semantic-class level across exact repeats: all 16 anchors had zero semantic PSD, mode flips, and semantic entropy. Exact repetition could still yield nonzero broader response-state variation, so observable wording, claims, actions, evidence use, uncertainty, assertion, referents, or abstention/request behavior should not be assumed perfectly fixed. Local perturbations mattered: paraphrasing increased full-state distance for 13 of 16 anchors (mean excess +0.0639), while prior-history context increased mean distance by +0.0450 and produced claim flips in 9.4% and action flips in 18.8% of matched pairs. Neither intervention caused semantic flips. Thus, the evidence supports bounded, condition-sensitive instability in broader observable response state, not meaningful instability of the semantic answer class. The proposed explanation that repetition or accumulation automatically hardens weak premises into stronger assertions should be rejected for this cohort: none of 10 eligible trajectories hardened. Prompt formulation and prior context should be retained as supported determinants of nonsemantic response-state changes."
